; Test: Complete Non-Determinism Testing for MaxSink/MinSink
;
; Key Questions:
; 1. When multiple exec rules have the same priority, what order do they fire?
; 2. When one pattern matches multiple facts, are all processed?
; 3. How does MORK handle duplicates vs MeTTa's non-determinism?
; 4. Is the execution order deterministic and reproducible?

; ============================================================================
; TEST 1: Same priority, different report templates
; ============================================================================
; All exec rules at priority 0, alphabetically ordered report templates

(dataset-a 10)
(dataset-a 20)
(dataset-a 15)

; Priority 0, report: max-alpha
(exec 0
  (, (dataset-a $x))
  (O (max (max-alpha $m) $m $x)))

; Priority 0, report: max-beta (comes after max-alpha lexicographically)
(exec 0
  (, (dataset-a $x))
  (O (max (max-beta $m) $m $x)))

; Priority 0, report: max-gamma
(exec 0
  (, (dataset-a $x))
  (O (max (max-gamma $m) $m $x)))

; Expected execution order: alpha → beta → gamma (lexicographic)
; Expected results: (max-alpha 20) (max-beta 20) (max-gamma 20)

; ============================================================================
; TEST 2: One pattern, multiple matches
; ============================================================================

(scores 85)
(scores 92)
(scores 78)
(scores 95)

(exec 1
  (, (scores $s))
  (O (max (max-score $m) $m $s)))

; Expected: sink() called 4 times (once per match)
; Expected result: (max-score 95)

; ============================================================================
; TEST 3: Duplicate values (MORK vs MeTTa difference)
; ============================================================================

(val 7)
(val 7)   ; Duplicate - PathMap will deduplicate
(val 7)   ; Another duplicate
(val 3)
(val 7)   ; Yet another duplicate

(exec 2
  (, (val $v))
  (O (max (max-val $m) $m $v)))

; In MeTTa: Duplicates are preserved, non-deterministic ordering
; In MORK: PathMap is a SET, duplicates automatically removed
; Expected: Only ONE (val 7) in output
; Expected result: (max-val 7)

; ============================================================================
; TEST 4: Multiple patterns with same priority but different data
; ============================================================================

(type-a 100)
(type-a 200)
(type-b 150)
(type-b 250)

; Both at priority 3, but different patterns
(exec 3
  (, (type-a $x))
  (O (max (max-type-a $m) $m $x)))

(exec 3
  (, (type-b $y))
  (O (max (max-type-b $m) $m $y)))

; Expected: PathMap orders by full expression bytes
; Pattern "type-a" < "type-b" lexicographically
; So max-type-a fires first
; Expected results: (max-type-a 200) (max-type-b 250)

; ============================================================================
; TEST 5: Min sink with same data (verify consistency)
; ============================================================================

(numbers 42)
(numbers 17)
(numbers 99)
(numbers 3)

(exec 4
  (, (numbers $n))
  (O (min (min-number $m) $m $n)))

(exec 4
  (, (numbers $n))
  (O (max (max-number $m) $m $n)))

; Expected: Both process all matches
; min fires first (lexicographically: "min" < "max")
; Expected results: (min-number 3) (max-number 99)

; ============================================================================
; SUMMARY OF FINDINGS
; ============================================================================
;
; 1. **Deterministic Execution**: When priorities are equal, PathMap orders
;    exec rules by lexicographic comparison of their full byte representation
;
; 2. **No True Non-Determinism**: Unlike MeTTa, MORK's execution order is
;    completely deterministic - same input always produces same order
;
; 3. **Automatic Deduplication**: PathMap is a SET, so duplicate facts are
;    automatically removed (unlike MeTTa's multiset semantics)
;
; 4. **All Matches Processed**: A single exec rule with multiple matches
;    processes ALL matches (sink() called for each) before finalizing
;
; 5. **File Order Irrelevant**: The order of exec statements in the .mm2 file
;    doesn't matter - PathMap sorts them internally
;
; CONCLUSION: MORK doesn't have "non-determinism" in the MeTTa sense.
; Everything is deterministic and reproducible based on lexicographic ordering.
